## Create an Azure SQL Database
# Make sure you add your IP address to the firewall rules.
# Select the Allow Azure services and resources to access this server to allow Github access

## Set up a GitHub repository
# Create a new private repo
# Clone the repo: https://github.com/Promise-Igbo/my-sql-db-repo.git

## Create and configure an Azure SQL Database project
# In the command palette, type Database projects: New and select it
# Select Azure SQL Database, Enter the name MyDBProj, and press Enter to confirm.
# Select the cloned GitHub repository folder to save the project.
# For SDK-style projects, select Yes (Recommended)

## Create a new SQL file in the project
# On Visual Studio Code, select the Database Projects icon located in the activity bar on the left.
# Right-click on your project name, and select Add Table.
# Name the table Employees and press Enter.
# Replace the existing script with the following code.
CREATE TABLE [dbo].[Employees]
(
    EmployeeID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    FirstName NVARCHAR(50),
    LastName NVARCHAR(50),
    Department NVARCHAR(50)
);

# Close the editor. Notice that the Employees.sql file is saved in the project.

## Commit the changes to the repository
# On Visual Studio Code, select the Source Control icon located in the activity bar on the left.
# Enter the message Created project and added a create table script.
# Select Commit to commit your changes.
# Under the ellipsis, select Push to push the changes to the repository.

## Verify the changes in the repository

## Set up Continuous Integration (CI) with GitHub Actions
# Add Secrets on your repository secret:
# AZURE_CREDENTIALS (or managed identity), AZURE_CONN_STRING: Server=<your_sqldb_server>.database.windows.net;Initial Catalog=MyDB;Persist Security Info=False;User ID=sqladmin;Password=<your_password>;Encrypt=True;Connection Timeout=30;

## Create a GitHub Actions workflow
# Select the set up a workflow yourself link: main.yml file
# Build and Deploy SQL Database Project workflow

## Verify the changes in the Azure SQL Database
# Navigate to the MyDB SQL Database.
# Select Query editor.
# Connect to the database using the sqladmin credentials.
# Under the Tables section, verify that the Employees table is created. Refresh if needed.